  "ai_usage": "Purely nostalgic decoration — use it on a retro/90s pastiche, a launch or waitlist page wanting a knowing wink, or a stats readout that benefits from an odometer feel. Do NOT use it as a serious analytics display; a rolling number is hard to read mid-animation and the format caps out at its padded width.\n\nIntegrate: put the target number in data-count on the counter element and set PAD to the digit width you want; the script zero-pads and rolls from zero on load with an ease-out. To show a real figure, fetch it and set data-count before calling roll() — do not animate on every poll, only when the value actually changes, or it never settles.\n\nTune --led / --led-off / --cabinet as a set: the unlit ghost should be a very dark tint of the lit colour, not grey, or it stops reading as an LCD. Keep the roll under about 1.2s; longer reads as broken rather than mechanical.\n\nAvoid: announcing intermediate values to screen readers (keep aria-live off and label the final number), rolling a number that changes constantly, and using the green-on-black as a text colour anywhere else — it is tuned for large digits, not body copy. Ship the reduced-motion guard; a spinning odometer is exactly the kind of motion it exists to stop.",
  "path": "effects/retro-web/visitor-counter.html",
  "source": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\" />\n  <title>Retro Visitor Counter</title>\n  <script type=\"application/json\" id=\"effect-meta\">\n  {\n    \"id\": \"visitor-counter\",\n    \"title\": \"Retro Visitor Counter\",\n    \"summary\": \"A 90s odometer hit counter in seven-segment digits, rolling up to its total with a CRT glow.\",\n    \"description\": \"The web-ring era's status symbol: a zero-padded odometer proving people had actually been here. The originals were server-rendered GIFs from a CGI script; this is a CSS rebuild — each digit is a seven-segment cell drawn with a monospace glyph and a lit/unlit colour pair, wrapped in a beveled cabinet with a scanline overlay and a phosphor glow. The count rolls up on load with an ease-out so it decelerates into its final value like a mechanical wheel losing momentum.\",\n    \"theme\": \"retro-web\",\n    \"categories\": [\"retro\", \"nostalgia\", \"counter\", \"decorative\"],\n    \"tags\": [\"retro\", \"90s\", \"visitor-counter\", \"hit-counter\", \"odometer\", \"seven-segment\", \"led\", \"crt\", \"scanlines\", \"geocities\", \"nostalgia\"],\n    \"tech\": [\"css\", \"js\", \"html\"],\n    \"era\": \"1990s\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"beginner\",\n    \"dependencies\": [],\n    \"browser_support\": \"All modern browsers. The glow uses text-shadow, not filter, so it stays cheap.\",\n    \"performance_notes\": \"One rAF loop for roughly a second at load, then completely idle. Only the digit text changes; the cabinet, bevel, and scanlines are static paint.\",\n    \"accessibility_notes\": \"The counter is wrapped in an aria-live=\\\"off\\\" region with a plain-text label, so a screen reader announces the final figure once rather than every intermediate frame. Under prefers-reduced-motion the roll-up is skipped and the final number is rendered immediately. The lit-digit colour clears 4.5:1 on the cabinet background.\",\n    \"customization\": [\n      { \"name\": \"--led\", \"description\": \"Lit segment colour\", \"default\": \"#37ff6d\" },\n      { \"name\": \"--led-off\", \"description\": \"Unlit segment ghost — keep it barely visible for the real LCD look\", \"default\": \"#123d1e\" },\n      { \"name\": \"--cabinet\", \"description\": \"Counter body colour\", \"default\": \"#0a0a0a\" },\n      { \"name\": \"data-count\", \"description\": \"The number to roll to; digit count is derived from its padded width\", \"default\": \"1048576\" },\n      { \"name\": \"PAD\", \"description\": \"How many digits to zero-pad to\", \"default\": \"7\" }\n    ],\n    \"variations\": [\n      \"Amber (#ffb000) or CRT-blue LEDs instead of green\",\n      \"Flip-digit split-flap styling rather than seven-segment\",\n      \"Add the obligatory \\\"You are visitor number\\\" banner and a hit-counter GIF border\",\n      \"Drive it from a real count and roll only the delta since last visit\"\n    ],\n    \"related\": [\"retro-90s-homepage\", \"marquee-scroll\", \"blink-text\"],\n    \"ai_usage\": \"Purely nostalgic decoration — use it on a retro/90s pastiche, a launch or waitlist page wanting a knowing wink, or a stats readout that benefits from an odometer feel. Do NOT use it as a serious analytics display; a rolling number is hard to read mid-animation and the format caps out at its padded width.\\n\\nIntegrate: put the target number in data-count on the counter element and set PAD to the digit width you want; the script zero-pads and rolls from zero on load with an ease-out. To show a real figure, fetch it and set data-count before calling roll() — do not animate on every poll, only when the value actually changes, or it never settles.\\n\\nTune --led / --led-off / --cabinet as a set: the unlit ghost should be a very dark tint of the lit colour, not grey, or it stops reading as an LCD. Keep the roll under about 1.2s; longer reads as broken rather than mechanical.\\n\\nAvoid: announcing intermediate values to screen readers (keep aria-live off and label the final number), rolling a number that changes constantly, and using the green-on-black as a text colour anywhere else — it is tuned for large digits, not body copy. Ship the reduced-motion guard; a spinning odometer is exactly the kind of motion it exists to stop.\"\n  }\n  </script>\n  <style>\n    :root {\n      --led: #37ff6d;\n      --led-off: #123d1e;\n      --cabinet: #0a0a0a;\n      --bezel: #2a2a2a;\n      --page: #1b1b22;\n    }\n    * { box-sizing: border-box; }\n    html, body { margin: 0; height: 100%; }\n    body {\n      background:\n        repeating-linear-gradient(45deg, #17171d 0 10px, #1b1b22 10px 20px);\n      color: #cfd4e0; font-family: Verdana, Geneva, system-ui, sans-serif;\n      display: grid; place-content: center; gap: .9rem; justify-items: center; padding: 1rem;\n    }\n    .label { font-size: .74rem; letter-spacing: .1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #9aa0b0; }\n\n    .counter {\n      display: flex; gap: 4px; padding: 10px 12px;\n      background: var(--cabinet);\n      border: 3px solid var(--bezel);\n      border-radius: 6px;\n      /* Chunky beveled cabinet: light top-left, dark bottom-right. */\n      box-shadow:\n        inset 2px 2px 0 #3d3d3d, inset -2px -2px 0 #000,\n        0 0 0 2px #000, 0 6px 18px #000a;\n      position: relative;\n    }\n    /* CRT scanlines over the digits. */\n    .counter::after {\n      content: \"\"; position: absolute; inset: 0; pointer-events: none;\n      border-radius: 3px;\n      background: repeating-linear-gradient(#0000 0 2px, #00000066 2px 3px);\n    }\n    .digit {\n      width: 30px; padding: 6px 0;\n      font-family: ui-monospace, \"DejaVu Sans Mono\", Menlo, monospace;\n      font-size: 2.1rem; line-height: 1; font-weight: 700; text-align: center;\n      color: var(--led);\n      background: #050805;\n      border: 1px solid #1d1d1d;\n      text-shadow: 0 0 6px var(--led), 0 0 14px #37ff6d66;\n      position: relative;\n    }\n    /* The unlit \"8\" ghosting behind every digit — the LCD tell. */\n    .digit::before {\n      content: \"8\"; position: absolute; inset: 6px 0;\n      color: var(--led-off); text-shadow: none;\n    }\n    .digit span { position: relative; }\n\n    .caption { font-size: .62rem; color: #6f7686; letter-spacing: .06em; }\n  </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <p class=\"label\">You are visitor number</p>\n  <div class=\"counter\" id=\"counter\" data-count=\"1048576\" aria-live=\"off\"></div>\n  <p class=\"caption\">since October 1998</p>\n\n  <script>\n    var PAD = 7, DURATION = 1100;\n    var el = document.getElementById(\"counter\");\n    var target = parseInt(el.getAttribute(\"data-count\"), 10) || 0;\n    var reduce = matchMedia(\"(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)\").matches;\n\n    function render(value) {\n      var str = String(Math.max(0, Math.round(value))).padStart(PAD, \"0\").slice(-PAD);\n      // Only the glyph changes; the cells themselves are built once.\n      for (var i = 0; i < PAD; i++) el.children[i].firstChild.textContent = str[i];\n    }\n    for (var i = 0; i < PAD; i++) {\n      var cell = document.createElement(\"div\");\n      cell.className = \"digit\";\n      cell.appendChild(document.createElement(\"span\"));\n      el.appendChild(cell);\n    }\n    // Label the settled figure for assistive tech, not every rolling frame.\n    el.setAttribute(\"aria-label\", target.toLocaleString(\"en-US\") + \" visitors\");\n\n    function roll(ts0) {\n      var start = null;\n      function step(ts) {\n        if (start === null) start = ts;\n        var t = Math.min((ts - start) / DURATION, 1);\n        var eased = 1 - Math.pow(1 - t, 3);   // ease-out cubic: decelerates like a wheel\n        render(target * eased);\n        if (t < 1) requestAnimationFrame(step);\n      }\n      requestAnimationFrame(step);\n    }\n\n    render(reduce ? target : 0);\n    if (!reduce) roll();\n  </script>\n</body>\n</html>\n"
}
